["We need to make 24 using numbers 8,5,5,9 each once, using + - * /. Find expression equals 24.\n\nTry combinations.\n\nWe can try (9-5/5)*8? Compute 5/5=1, 9-1=8, *8 =64. Too high.\n\n(9+5/5)*? 5/5=1, 9+1=10, need *? 8? 10*8=80.\n\n(9-5)* (8-5) =4*3=12.\n\n(9+5)*(8/5) =14*1.6=22.4.\n\n(9*5 -5)/? 9*5=45-5=40. Need /? 8? 40/8=5.\n\n(9*5 -8)/5 = (45-8)/5=37/5=7.4.\n\n(8*5 -5)/? 8*5=40-5=35. Need /9? 35/9≈3.889.\n\n(9*8)/(5-5?) denominator zero.\n\n(9+8)*(5/5)=17*1=17.\n\n(9*5)+(8/5)=45+1.6=46.6.\n\nTry using subtraction after division: 9* (5-5/8) = 9*(5-0.625)=9*4.375=39.375.\n\nTry (8/(5-9/5))? Compute 9/5=1.8, 5-1.8=3.2, 8/3.2=2.5.\n\nTry (9/(5-8/5))? 8/5=1.6, 5-1.6=3.4, 9/3.4≈2.647.\n\nTry (5*5)-(9-8)=25-1=24! Yes: 5*5 - (9-8) = 25 - 1 = 24. Uses each number once. Operators allowed + - * /. That's valid.\n\nExpression: 5*5-(9-8)\n\nWe need no '=' sign. Use '*' for multiplication, '-' for subtraction. That's fine.\n\nWrap in <answer>5*5-(9-8)</answer>"]